Start and use a local development server for overriding flag values.

Usage:
  ldcli dev-server [command]

Project commands:
  add-project      add a project
  get-project      get a project
  import-project   import project from file
  list-projects    list all projects
  remove-project   remove a project
  sync-project     sync project
  update-project   update project

Override commands:
  add-override     override flag value
  remove-override  remove override
  remove-overrides remove all overrides for a project

Server commands:
  start            start the dev server
  ui               open the ui

Optional flags:
      --cors-enabled            Enable CORS headers for browser-based
                                developer tools (default: false)
      --cors-origin string      Allowed CORS origin. Use '*' for all
                                origins (default: '*') (default "*")
      --dev-stream-uri string   Streaming service endpoint that the dev
                                server uses to obtain authoritative flag
                                data. This may be a LaunchDarkly or Relay
                                Proxy endpoint (default
                                "https://stream.launchdarkly.com")
      --port string             Port for the dev server to run on (default
                                "8765")

Global flags:
  -h, --help                  Get help about any command
      --access-token string   LaunchDarkly access token with write-level access
      --analytics-opt-out     Opt out of analytics tracking
      --base-uri string       LaunchDarkly base URI (default "https://app.launchdarkly.com")
      --fields strings        Comma-separated list of top-level fields to include in JSON output (e.g., --fields key,name,kind)
      --json                  Output JSON format (shorthand for --output json)
  -o, --output string         Output format: json, plaintext, or markdown (default: plaintext in a terminal, json otherwise) (default "json")

Use "ldcli dev-server [command] --help" for more information about a command.
